<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Pulitzer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pulitzer József</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Pulitzer was a Hungarian-American politician and newspaper publisher of the St. Louis Post-Dispatch and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> World. Born in Makó, he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1864 and fought for the Union in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, then settled in St. Louis, where he formed the Post-Dispatch in 1878; he bought the World in 1883. In the 1890s its rivalry with William Randolph Hearst's New York Journal produced the sensational methods of yellow journalism. He served one term in Congress. His will endowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>, whose School of Journalism opened in 1912 and whose Pulitzer Prizes were established in 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-pulitzer.jpg" alt="Joseph Pulitzer" width="328" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JosephPulitzerPinceNeznpsgov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Pulitzer was the son of Fülöp Pulitzer, a respected merchant of Makó whose family belonged to the Jewish community there. In 1853 the father retired and moved the family to Pest. After his death in 1858 the business went bankrupt and the family became poor. The Austrian Army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-foreign-legion">French Foreign Legion</a> and the British Army all rejected Pulitzer, and in August 1864 he was recruited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> to fight for the Union. He reached Boston Harbor at 17 speaking no English, served in the 1st New York Cavalry Regiment and was mustered out on 5 June 1865. In 1870 he won a seat in the Missouri legislature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 9 December 1878 Pulitzer bought the moribund St. Louis Dispatch and merged it with John Dillon's St. Louis Post. Under his control the Post-Dispatch championed the common man with exposés, and its circulation rose from under 4,000 at the merger to 22,300 by September 1882. In 1883 he bought the New York World from the railroad magnate Jay Gould for $346,000. Elected to the House of Representatives in 1884, he resigned on 10 April 1886. After William Randolph Hearst bought the New York Journal in 1895, the two began a circulation war, and their coverage before and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> linked Pulitzer's name with yellow journalism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Blindness, depression and acute sensitivity to noise forced Pulitzer out of daily management. He died on 29 October 1911 aboard his yacht in Charleston Harbor and was buried in Woodlawn Cemetery in the Bronx. In 1892 he had offered money to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> for a school of journalism, which it first declined. His will left the university $2,000,000. The Columbia University Graduate School of Journalism was founded in 1912, and the first Pulitzer Prizes were awarded in 1917.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Pulitzer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joseph Pulitzer</b>'s newspaper, the New York World, then ran a drive that by 11 August 1885 had raised 102,000 dollars from 120,000 donors, 80 percent of it in sums under a dollar.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
